// @test: verify-pass
// @level: L3
// @tier: 0
// @tags: verification, smt, refinement-reflection, proofs
// @timeout: 30000
// @description: A predicate this translator cannot express still has a
//   NAME the solver knows, so claims that follow from being a function
//   are provable about it.
//
// Two different things can go wrong with a reflection candidate, and
// until T0905 both answered the same way — nothing at all:
//
//   DETERMINISM      the function may return different results for the
//                    same arguments (contexts, effects). Nothing true
//                    can be said about it, and nothing is.
//   TRANSLATABILITY  the function is a perfectly good mathematical
//                    function; this translator cannot express its BODY.
//                    A loop is the ordinary case.
//
// The second kind still supports one true statement — `f` is a
// function, so `f(x)` equals itself — and `(declare-fun f (S…) S)` is
// exactly that statement. Emitting nothing did not make the name
// UNINTERPRETED, which is how the code described it; it made the name
// UNDECLARED, which is a different and worse thing. A goal mentioning
// an undeclared symbol fails on WELL-FORMEDNESS, and that failure is
// reported in the same shape as a failed proof — so "this property is
// not provable" and "this file does not typecheck for the solver" were
// indistinguishable in the output.
//
// Nothing here asks the solver to know what the loop COMPUTES. Each
// claim below follows from `sum_to` being a function and from nothing
// else, which is the whole of what a declaration asserts.
//
// `_unsound.vr` is the pole, and it carries TWO kinds of failure that
// must be told apart — see its header.

/// The body is a `while` loop: not a `let`, not an assignment to a
/// local, not an early-return guard, so the reflector's straight-line
/// fold declines it. Only Int arithmetic, so that a failure here can
/// only be about the loop and not about lists, iterators or generics.
///
/// `pure` is load-bearing and is NOT decoration. A declaration asserts
/// determinism, so the opaque path emits one only for a function
/// declared `pure`. The definitional path never had that gate — impure
/// functions are excluded from it only INCIDENTALLY, by their bodies
/// failing to translate — which is precisely the accident this change
/// would have removed had the gate not been added with it.
public pure fn sum_to(n: Int) -> Int {
    let mut acc: Int = 0;
    let mut i: Int = 0;
    while i < n {
        acc = acc + i;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    acc
}

/// Excluded middle over the predicate form. True for any declared
/// symbol of Bool sort and unstatable about a symbol that does not
/// exist — which makes it the narrowest possible witness that the
/// declaration is emitted.
public pure fn reaches(n: Int) -> Bool {
    let mut i: Int = 0;
    while i < n {
        i = i + 1;
    }
    i >= n
}

@verify(formal)
public theorem an_untranslatable_predicate_still_has_excluded_middle(n: Int)
    ensures reaches(n) || !reaches(n)
    proof by auto;

/// A SECOND CONTROL, and it is here because it looked like a witness
/// and is not.
///
/// `sum_to(n) == sum_to(n)` reads as "determinism, said directly — the
/// entire content of a `declare-fun`". Measured on the binary that
/// predates this change, it ALREADY PROVED. The goal translator renders
/// a call as `(name args…)` keyed on the name, so both sides are the
/// same term and congruence closure closes it without any declaration
/// existing.
///
/// Kept, relabelled, rather than deleted: it marks the boundary between
/// what a declaration buys and what term identity gives for free, and
/// the next person to reach for "f(x) == f(x)" as evidence that a
/// symbol exists needs to find this note instead of repeating the
/// measurement. The two theorems that DID move are the ones above and
/// below.
@verify(formal)
public theorem term_identity_needs_no_declaration(n: Int)
    ensures sum_to(n) == sum_to(n)
    proof by auto;

/// THE SECOND EFFECT, and the one that reaches real modules.
///
/// The registry closes itself under its call graph and drops any entry
/// whose body names a symbol the block never declares (T0489) — one
/// bad axiom must degrade one function, not poison the module. But an
/// un-translatable helper WAS such an undeclared symbol, so every
/// caller of one was dropped too, losing an axiom that translated
/// perfectly well. Declaring the helper closes the graph and the
/// caller survives.
///
/// `negated` is fully translatable; it is here only as something that
/// calls `reaches`.
public pure fn negated(n: Int) -> Bool {
    !reaches(n)
}

@verify(formal)
public theorem a_caller_of_an_opaque_helper_keeps_its_own_axiom(n: Int)
    ensures negated(n) == !reaches(n)
    proof by auto;

/// The control: a fully translatable predicate, no loop anywhere. It
/// proved before this change and must keep proving. Without it, a
/// change that emptied the reflector entirely would still let every
/// assertion above pass for the wrong reason — an undeclared symbol
/// and a declared one both admit excluded middle if the goal itself is
/// never reached.
public fn direct(n: Int) -> Bool {
    n + 0 == n
}

@verify(formal)
public theorem the_translatable_control_still_proves(n: Int)
    ensures direct(n)
    proof by auto;

fn main() {
    print("opaque-untranslatable");
}
